1. What is encapsulation?
    Encapsulation means keeping a class’s data (fields) and the methods that operate on that
data together, while hiding the data from outside code. Other classes can’t read or change
the fields directly. They have to go through public methods that you control.

    The usual way to do it is:
        Make fields private.
        Provide public methods (getters, setters, or behavior methods) to access them.

2. Why do we use it?
    - Data protection
    - Validation in one place: the rules live inside the class, so every caller follows
    them automatically.
    - Flexibility: you can change the internal implementation (say, store marks as int
    instead of double) without breaking other code.
    - Easier debugging: if a value is wrong, only the class’s own methods could have changed it.
    - Read-only or write-only control: you decide what is exposed.

3. Access modifiers

Access modifiers decide who can see a class member. They are the tool that makes
encapsulation possible.

Modifier	    Same class	    Same package	Subclass (other package)	Anywhere
private	            Yes	            No	               No	                    No
default(none)       Yes	            Yes	               No	                    No
protected	        Yes	            Yes	               Yes	                    No
public	             Yes	        Yes	               Yes	                    Yes

- private: only inside the same class. Use it for fields.
- default: no keyword. Visible only inside the same package.
- protected: same package, plus subclasses even in other packages. Used for inheritance.
- public: visible everywhere. Use it for the methods you want others to call.

